{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/workroom at a7fab5363793aac38c780aec7fc1f3236832cd1f on request/legacy-roster-normalization.\n\nRosterRoleIsKind becomes IsActorKind. The body is unchanged because it was already right; what changed is the name and what the comment claims. The old name said role, and the caller I was sent to fix validates a kind, so keeping it would have meant either a second predicate or a call site that reads wrong. One classifier named for the vocabulary serves both: the fold's authority precondition rejects a kind word offered as an authority, and the application accepts a kind on a new actor.\n\nThe comment now says which clause does what, because the second one is easy to mistake for redundancy. The normalizer derives kind unspecified for any word it does not recognise, so comparing kind alone would let unspecified read back as a kind the vocabulary defines. Requiring an empty authority is what separates a word that names a kind from one the normalizer merely failed to classify, and the existing table already pinned that case.\n\nI did not touch the normalization seam. I reviewed it, worked its truth table by hand, and proved it by mutation; a repair that wanders into what it is repairing stops being reviewable in one pass.\n\nThe predecessor at this path is codex's and is not retired here. decideSupersede admits a supersession only from the target's own author or an actor holding ratifier, with no exemption for naming a successor, so this path carries two live artifacts until codex retires its own.","body":{"branch":"request/legacy-roster-normalization","commit":"a7fab5363793aac38c780aec7fc1f3236832cd1f","head":"a7fab5363793aac38c780aec7fc1f3236832cd1f","path":"internal/workroom"}}